hink have gotten a job yeah um  what are some of like the tips or tricks  that you tell folks in terms of like  they can increase the probability of  success in transitioning yeah there's a  number of things one is just find out  what you're passionate about uh you know  if you're a bitcoin only then go look  for a bitcoin only company so number one  is just kind of do your research  beforehand identify what kind of role  you're looking for if you're someone  who's only worked in customer experience  like don't go looking for a software  developer job your chances of success  there are next to zero  and the other thing is don't apply to a  bunch of different roles at companies  i say them laugh because i'm 25 i  recently graduated college when i  graduated i had no clue what i wanted to  do so i would literally just go on  linkedin and like look at companies that  were interesting you know nike under  armour whatever  and go apply to like 10 different roles  at the companies  turns out that's a pretty ineffective  strategy  so kind of narrow down what you're  looking for and kind of how to align  with your skill set  if your skill set isn't suited towards  what you want to do then take the  initiative on your own and go you know  do a boot camp go build up some  experience build up a portfolio that you  can kind of showcase to these companies  and that's like the most interesting  thing so the advice i always give job  seekers is like do the job before you  get the job right which is if you want  to be a salesperson if you show up to  the interview and you're like have these  three leads that i found for you guys  they want to talk to someone here at the  company who should i introduce them to  if you're the person hiring like i think  you're going to be pretty good at this  like you know we should we should find a  role for you but also there's other  things where uh in some way it used to  be like resumes mattered so what school  did you go to what was your degree did  you what was your gpa like all that type  of stuff now if somebody shows up and  like i got 50 000 followers on twitter  and i talk only about i don't know  customer service uh you know uh best  practices  a customer service uh role you're likely  to be pretty good at it right like  you've shown the the proficiency in it  or the knowledge base like what have you  seen people do there that's pretty cool  yeah what i think it comes down to is  like de-risking the hire  so when you come with a portfolio or you  come with leads you know the hiring  manager wants to see that you can do the  job and so i've been on both sides i've  applied and then  i've hired and the people that really  show the initiative to write the cover  letter to show genuine interest in the  company and then come prepared  you know to the interview here's my  portfolio here are some leads it really  you know lets the hiring manager know  that the person can do the job well yeah  when you start to think about uh things  people can do to learn more like where  do you normally send folks from like a  learning just hey i need to get my  experience and my knowledge base up  before i actually start to apply yeah if  you want to work in the industry i think  you have to be on twitter i think that's  step number one and then from there  start with the free resources you know  follow people like you follow people  like sailor  follow the people that are pushing out  you know quality content uh you know go  down the youtube rabbit hole watch  different podcasts sign up for  newsletters just kind of absorb as much  free content as you can first and then  once you kind of have that baseline  level understanding that's when i think  you can go you know take the next step  continue to network we have our training  program that's really good for  networking and getting right in front of  the right people at companies um the  cryptoacademy.io  and we have a career day where these  companies literally come in they pitch  students uh on why they should come work  for them they literally tell them hey  here's what we look for in applicants  here's what you should and shouldn't do  in the application process  and just really puts you kind of ahead  of of the curve  in comparison to to other applicants and  so when you see people  be successful how long does this take is  this a  one week process it does take a whole  year like what would you say the average  transition time is yeah it really just  depends we've seen people go through our  program specifically and then land a job  within a couple of days  and there's some people that you know  they couldn't leave their job you know  for a couple months to even potentially  a couple years  um one guy comes to mind he works now  across the river he was formerly at usaa  uh he was in like our third cohort which  is basically was a year ago  and um he wasn't able to leave his job  he wanted to stay there for whatever  reasons he couldn't relocate etc  and then only recently he accepted a  sweet job at cross river twitter has  almost become linkedin to some degree uh  what do you see people do there  successfully in terms of using that  platform yeah i hate to to my own horn  but uh i have a great story there but i  think just being yourself on twitter um  you know if you're a meme guy create  memes if you're a content guy you know  put out quality threads and things like  that  but the thing that's unique about  twitter is it allows you to essentially  kind of be in the same virtual room as  super successful people that you  wouldn't otherwise be in rooms with  so you can you know dm people you can at  people and you know it's really just the  ultimate networking tool in my opinion  and in crypto specifically people are a  lot more receptive to responding to  stuff as opposed to linkedin myself  included if someone adds me on linkedin  they send me a message i'm terrible at  responding to it but i'm much better at  responding 